            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Weekly Observation: Surge in Appeals, Extended Review Cycles</h2>
<p>Based on feedback from multiple overseas marketing communities, Google Ads account suspensions increased significantly this week. Many advertisers reported that the waiting time after submitting appeals extended from the usual 3-5 business days to over a week. One utility app team mentioned receiving account suspension notice on Tuesday morning, with their appeal submitted Friday still unreplied.</p>
<p>This situation relates to Google's recent tightening of compliance reviews. As advertisers concentrate their budgets for year-end spending, the platform's risk control thresholds are dynamically adjusting.</p>
<h2>Two High-Frequency Suspension Reasons</h2>
<p><strong>Inaccessible or non-compliant landing pages</strong> is currently the most common suspension reason. Specific manifestations include: pages taking over 3 seconds to load, image or video elements failing, exaggerated promotional language on brand websites, etc. One cross-border e-commerce team had their advertising qualification suspended due to missing return and exchange policy information on their promotional page.</p>
<p><strong>Payment method anomalies</strong> rank second. Common issues include: credit card holder information not matching account registration, multiple accounts sharing the same payment method, insufficient balance in prepaid accounts triggering risk controls, etc.</p>
<h2>Appeal Materials Preparation Checklist</h2>
<p>For the above issues, it is recommended to prepare appeal materials in the following steps:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Step 1: Confirm Violation Type</strong><br />
Log into Google Ads backend and check specific violation descriptions in the "Policies and Legal" page. Take screenshots for preservation—this is an essential attachment for appeals.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Make Targeted Fixes</strong><br />
If it's a landing page issue, immediately repair the webpage content to ensure mobile accessibility and complete display of key information.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Prepare Appeal Form</strong><br />
When submitting an account restoration request through Google Ads Help Center, you need to fill in:<br />
- Account ID and company name<br />
- Violation type description<br />
- Corrective measures already taken<br />
- Commitment to future compliance plan</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Attach Supporting Documents</strong><br />
It is recommended to attach: landing page screenshots, server access logs, brand qualification documents, etc., to enhance persuasiveness.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>This Week's Impact Assessment</h2>
<p>For overseas companies relying on Google Ads for traffic, account suspension means traffic interruption and conversion losses. Take one e-commerce independent website as an example, which normally receives about 30% of orders through Google Ads. During the account suspension period, daily sales dropped significantly. This reminds us that account risk control should be incorporated into daily operational preventive management, rather than reactive responses.</p>
<h2>Recommended Actions</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Proactive Inspection</strong>: Check landing page accessibility and content compliance weekly</li>
<li><strong>Payment Isolation</strong>: Try to configure independent payment methods for each Google Ads account</li>
<li><strong>Alternative Channels</strong>: Set up advertising accounts on Meta, TikTok, and other platforms in advance to reduce single-platform dependency</li>
<li><strong>Appeal Records</strong>: Keep all appeal correspondence records for follow-up or escalation if needed</li>
</ol>
<p>If no response is received after more than 10 business days of appeal, it is recommended to follow up through Google Ads official Twitter account or partner support channels.</p>
